<article class="env-lemma" id="054J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/054J">Lemma <span data-tag="054J">29.23.2</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is quasi-compact and locally of finite presentation, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$Y$ is quasi-compact and quasi-separated. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then the image of every constructible subset of $X$ is constructible in $Y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/054E" data-tag="054E">28.2.5</a> it suffices to prove this lemma in case $Y$ is affine. In this case $X$ is quasi-compact. Hence we can write $X = U_1 \cup \ldots \cup U_ n$ with each $U_ i$ affine open in $X$. If $E \subset X$ is constructible, then each $E \cap U_ i$ is constructible too, see Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/005J" data-tag="005J">5.15.4</a>. Hence, since $f(E) = \bigcup f(E \cap U_ i)$ and since finite unions of constructible sets are constructible, this reduces us to the case where $X$ is affine. In this case the result is Algebra, Theorem <a href="/tag/00FE" data-tag="00FE">10.29.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>